efore, these points are additional intersection points where lines from \u03c01 and lines from \u03c02 intersect on L.\n\nHowever, each such point is the intersection of a line on \u03c01 (from plane A) and a line on \u03c02 (from plane A). Wait, plane A intersects \u03c01 along line L_A1 and \u03c02 along line L_A2. These two lines, L_A1 and L_A2, both lie on plane A and intersect at the point where plane A meets line L (the intersection of \u03c01 and \u03c02). Therefore, each plane A contributes an intersection point on L where L_A1 and L_A2 meet. However, these points are already counted in the 15 + 15 points on \u03c01 and \u03c02.\n\nWait, this is getting too tangled. Let me approach this differently.\n\nIf we have 8 planes: \u03c01, \u03c02, and six others (A, B, C, D, E, F). Each of the six others intersect \u03c01 and \u03c02 along lines. So, on \u03c01, we have six lines (from A, B, C, D, E, F), leading to C(6,2)=15 intersection points. Similarly, on \u03c02, six lines leading to 15 intersection points.\n\nAdditionally, the six planes A-F can form trios among themselves, each trio contributing an intersection point not on \u03c01 or \u03c02. That's C(6,3)=20 points.\n\nAdditionally, trios involving \u03c01 or \u03c02 with two of the other planes. Each such trio's intersection point is already on \u03c01 or \u03c02, counted in the 15 + 15.\n\nAdditionally, trios involving both \u03c01 and \u03c02 with one other plane. As mentioned, these trios intersect along line L (the intersection of \u03c01 and \u03c02), at the point where the other plane intersects L. Since each of the six planes intersects L at a unique point, we get six points on L. However, each of these points is the intersection of a line on \u03c01 and a line on \u03c02. For example, plane A intersects \u03c01 along line L_A1 and \u03c02 along line L_A2. These two lines intersect at point P_A on L. Similarly for planes B-F, leading to six points on L.\n\nHowever, are these points already counted 