ad 36^2 = 27 \\times 48 = 1296\n\\]\nAll conditions are satisfied. Therefore, the value of \\( b \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\n1. Let's introduce the variables and their conditions. We have three positive integers \\(a, b,\\) and \\(c\\) satisfying the following:\n   \\[\n   1 < a < b < c, \\quad a + b + c = 111, \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = ac.\n   \\]\n\n2. Let\u2019s express \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) using the greatest common divisor (gcd). Suppose \\((a, c) = d\\), meaning \\(d\\) is the gcd of \\(a\\) and \\(c\\). We can write:\n   \\[\n   a = a_1d, \\quad c = c_1d,\n   \\]\n   where \\(a_1\\) and \\(c_1\\) are coprime integers (\\(\\gcd(a_1, c_1) = 1\\)).\n\n3. Substitute these into the equation \\(b^2 = ac\\):\n   \\[\n   b^2 = (a_1 d)(c_1 d) = d^2 a_1 c_1.\n   \\]\n   Hence, we have \\(d^2\\) dividing \\(b^2\\), implying that \\(d\\) must divide \\(b\\). Let \\(b = b_1 d\\), where \\(b_1\\) is an integer.\n\n4. Substitute \\(b = b_1 d\\) into the equation \\(b^2 = d^2 a_1 c_1\\):\n   \\[\n   (b_1 d)^2 = d^2 a_1 c_1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b_1^2 d^2 = d^2 a_1 c_1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b_1^2 = a_1 c_1.\n   \\]\n\n5. Since \\(a_1\\) and \\(c_1\\) are coprime and their product is a perfect square (because \\(b_1^2\\) is a square), both \\(a_1\\) and \\(c_1\\) must be perfect squares. Suppose:\n   \\[\n   a_1 = m^2, \\quad c_1 = n^2,\n   \\]\n   where \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are integers such that \\(\\gcd(m, n) = 1\\) and \\(m < n\\).\n\n6. Thus, \\(b_1 = mn\\). Rewriting the equation \\(a + b + c = 111\\) with the new expressions:\n   \\[\n   a = m^2 d, \\quad b = b_1 d = mnd, \\quad c = n^2 d,\n   \\]\n   we obtain:\n   \\[\n   m^2 d + mn d + n^2 d = 111 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad d(m^2 + mn + n^2) = 111.\n   \\]\n\n7. We need to find \\(d, m,\\) and \\(n\\) such that \\(d(m^2 + mn + n^2) = 111\\). Since \\(m^2 + mn + n^2 \\geq 7\\) (because \\(m \\geq 1, n \\geq 2\\)) and 111 is factorizable, we inspect possible values for \\(d\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   d &= 1 & \\text{then} & & m^2 + mn + n^2 &= 111, \\\\\n   d &= 3 & \\text{then} & & m^2 + mn + n^2 &= 111 / 3 = 37.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   For \\(d = 1\\), it is hard to find integers \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) such that \\(m^2 + mn + n^2 = 111\\). The trial values do not satisfy the condition \\(1 < a < b < c\\). \n   \n   Let's check \\(d = 3\\):\n   \\[\n   m^2 + mn + n^2 = 37.\n   \\]\n   After trying different values:\n   \\[\n   m = 3, \\quad n = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 3^2 + 3 \\cdot 4 + 4^2 = 9 + 12 + 16 = 37.\n   \\]\n\n8. Substituting \\(m = 3\\) and \\(n = 4\\) back into the original equations, we get:\n   \\[\n   a = m^2 d = 3^2 \\cdot 3 = 27, \\quad b = m n d = 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 = 36, \\quad c = n^2 d = 4^2 \\cdot 3 = 48.\n   \\]\n   So:\n   \\[\n   a + b + c = 27 + 36 + 48 = 111.\n   \\]\n\n9. All conditions are satisfied with \\(d = 3\\), \\(m = 3\\), and \\(n = 4\\). Thus, the value of \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}.\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Two points \\( A \\) and \\( C \\) are marked on a circle; assume the tangents to the circle at \\( A \\) and \\( C \\) meet at \\( P \\). Let \\( B \\) be another point on the circle, and suppose \\( PB \\) meets the circle again at \\( D \\). Show that \\( AB \\cdot CD = BC \\cdot DA \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that AB multiplied by CD equals BC multiplied by DA. Let me try to visualize this first. There's a circle with points A and C on it. The tangents at A and C meet at point P. Then there's another point B on the circle, and when we connect P to B, this line PB intersects the circle again at D. The goal is to prove that AB\u00b7CD = BC\u00b7DA.\n\nHmm, maybe I should start by drawing a diagram. Since I can't actually draw it, I'll try to imagine it. Points A and C on a circle, tangents at those points meet at P. So P is outside the circle, and PA and PC are tangent segments. Then point B is somewhere else on the circle, and connecting P to B gives me a secant that intersects the circle again at D. \n\nI remember that when dealing with tangents and secants, there are some power of a point theorems that might be useful here. The power of point P with respect to the circle should be equal to the square of the tangent length. So PA^2 = PC^2 = PB\u00b7PD, because PB is a secant line passing through P and intersecting the circle at B and D. That might come into play.\n\nSo PA = PC since both are tangents from the same external point P to the circle. That's a property of tangents: they are equal in length from a common external point. So PA = PC.\n\nNow, the equation we need to show is AB\u00b7CD = BC\u00b7DA. That looks like a proportionality statement. Maybe similar triangles are involved here? Or perhaps some cyclic quadrilateral properties?\n\nAlternatively, cross ratios or harmonic divisions? Hmm. Let's see. If I can find similar triangles where the sides AB, BC, CD, DA are in proportion, that might work. Let me try to find some triangles in the figure that might be similar.\n\nLooking at points A, B, C, D on the circle. Since they are all on the circle, maybe some inscribed angles can be related. Let me recall that angles subtended by the same chord are equal. Also, angles in the same segment are equal.\n\nWait, maybe if I consider triangles involving P. For instance, triangle PAB and triangle PCD? But I'm not sure if those are similar. Alternatively, maybe triangles PAB and PDA?\n\nAlternatively, since PA and PC are tangents, the angles between the tangents and the lines PA and PC might be equal to the angles in the alternate segments. That's the alternate segment theorem. For example, the angle between tangent PA and chord AB is equal to the angle in the alternate segment, which would be angle ACB or something like that.\n\nLet me recall the alternate segment theorem: the angle between a tangent and a chord at t