uted using the cross products of vectors formed by these vertices. Each triangular face's area is half the magnitude of the cross product of the vectors forming its edges. The cross products for each face are:\n\n1. **Face \\(A_1BC_1\\)**: \\((\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{c}) \\times (\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b})\\)\n2. **Face \\(A_1BD\\)**: \\((\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{c}) \\times (\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{c})\\)\n3. **Face \\(A_1C_1D\\)**: \\((\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) \\times (\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{c})\\)\n4. **Face \\(BC_1D\\)**: \\((\\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c}) \\times (\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a})\\)\n\nEach cross product results in a combination of \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\), \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\) with different signs. When we compute the squared magnitudes of these cross products and sum them, the cross terms cancel out due to their opposite signs. The remaining terms sum to four times the sum of the squares of the original cross products:\n\n\\[\n4(\\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\|^2 + \\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\|^2 + \\|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\|^2)\n\\]\n\nSince each triangular face's area is half the magnitude of these cross products, the sum of the squares of the areas of the tetrahedron's faces is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\left( 4(\\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\|^2 + \\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\|^2 + \\|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\|^2) \\right) = \\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\|^2 + \\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\|^2 + \\|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\|^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the squares of the areas of the three non-parallel faces of the parallelepiped is equal to the sum of the squares of the areas of the faces of the tetrahedron \\(A_1BC_1D\\). The final result is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sum_{\\text{three faces}} \\text{Area}^2 = \\sum_{\\text{tetrahedron faces}} \\text{Area}^2}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Define the Vectors for the Tetrahedron**:\n   Let's denote the vectors of the parallelepiped as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{a} = \\overrightarrow{A_{1}B}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} = \\overrightarrow{BC_{1}}, \\quad \\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{C_{1}D}\n   \\]\n   These vectors correspond to the edges of the tetrahedron \\(A_{1}BC_{1}D\\).\n\n2. **Areas of Faces of the Tetrahedron**:\n   The areas of the faces of the tetrahedron can be calculated using the cross products of these vectors:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } A_{1}BC_{1} = \\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } BC_{1}D = \\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } C_{1}DA_{1} = \\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } A_{1}BD = \\frac{1}{2}|(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c}|\n   \\]\n\n3. **Doubled Areas**:\n   The doubled areas of the tetrahedron faces are given by:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}| = |\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}| = |\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}| = |\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}|(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c}| = |(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c}|\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sum of Squares of the Areas**:\n   Sum the squares of the areas for the tetrahedron:\n   \\[\n   (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})^2 + (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 + (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a})^2 + ((\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c})^2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Areas of Parallelepiped**:\n   For the parallelepiped, the areas of the faces can be represented using the same vectors:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of face } ABCD = |\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of face } AB_{1}D_{1} = |\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of face } BC_{1}D_{1} = |\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}|\n   \\]\n   \n6. **Sum of Squares of the Areas for Parallelepiped**:\n   Sum the squares of the areas of the parallelepiped:\n   \\[\n   (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})^2 + (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 + (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2\n   \\]\n\n7. **Equating Both Sums**:\n   From the problem, the sum of the squares of the areas of the three mutually non-parallel faces of the parallelepiped is equal to the corresponding sum for the tetrahedron:\n   \\[\n   (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})^2 + (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 + (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 = (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})^2 + (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 + (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a})^2 + ((\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c})^2\n   \\]\n\n8. **Verification**:\n   Each sum on the left of the equation represents the double areas of the parallelepiped faces, while on the right, those of the tetrahedron's faces. It rationally stands that:\n   \\[\n   |(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c}| = |\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}| + |\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}|\n   \\]\n\n#### Conclusion:\nThe sum of the squares of the areas of the three mutually non-parallel faces of the parallelepiped is equal to the sum of the squares of the areas of the faces of the tetrahedron. Therefore,\n\\[\n\\boxed{ (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})^2 + (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 + (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 = (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})^2 + (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 + (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a})^2 + ((\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c})^2 }\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "What is the probability of getting a sum of 30 when rolling a fair six-sided die 10 times, where the probability of each face (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6) is $\\frac{1}{6}$?", "deepseek_reasoning": "O