f the twentieth term is also a natural number.\n\n1. **Understanding the GP Terms**:\n   - The first term \\( a \\) is a natural number.\n   - The tenth term \\( a \\cdot r^9 \\) is a natural number.\n   - The thirtieth term \\( a \\cdot r^{29} \\) is a natural number.\n\n2. **Rationality of \\( r \\)**:\n   - Since \\( a \\cdot r^9 \\) and \\( a \\cdot r^{29} \\) are natural numbers, \\( r^9 \\) and \\( r^{29} \\) must be rational numbers.\n   - The ratio \\( \\frac{a \\cdot r^{29}}{a \\cdot r^9} = r^{20} \\) is rational, implying \\( r^{20} \\) is rational.\n   - Since \\( r^9 \\) and \\( r^{20} \\) are rational, \\( r \\) must be rational. This is because if \\( r^2 \\) (from \\( r^{20} = (r^{20/9})^9 \\)) and \\( r^9 \\) are rational, \\( r \\) itself must be rational.\n\n3. **Form of \\( r \\)**:\n   - Let \\( r = \\frac{p}{q} \\) where \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are coprime integers.\n   - For \\( a \\cdot \\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)^9 \\) and \\( a \\cdot \\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)^{29} \\) to be natural, \\( a \\) must be a multiple of \\( q^{29} \\). Thus, \\( a = k \\cdot q^{29} \\) for some natural number \\( k \\).\n\n4. **Twentieth Term Calculation**:\n   - The twentieth term is \\( a \\cdot r^{19} = k \\cdot q^{29} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)^{19} = k \\cdot p^{19} \\cdot q^{10} \\).\n   - Since \\( k \\), \\( p \\), and \\( q \\) are natural numbers, the twentieth term \\( k \\cdot p^{19} \\cdot q^{10} \\) is a natural number.\n\n5. **Verification with Examples**:\n   - Examples with specific values of \\( a \\), \\( p \\), and \\( q \\) confirm that the twentieth term is always a natural number.\n\nThus, the conditions given ensure that the twentieth term of the geometric progression is a natural number.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Yes}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let $a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots, a_{n}, \\ldots$ be the given geometric progression, where $q$ is the common ratio.\n\n2. According to the problem, the first, tenth, and thirtieth terms are natural numbers:\n   \\[ a_{1}, \\, a_{10} = a_{1} q^{9}, \\, a_{30} = a_{1} q^{29} \\]\n\n3. Since $a_{1}$, $a_{10}$, and $a_{30}$ are natural numbers, $q^{9}$ and $q^{29}$ must be positive rational numbers.\n\n4. We can express $q^2$ in terms of $q^{9}$ and $q^{29}$:\n   \\[\n   q^{2} = \\frac{q^{29}}{\\left(q^{9}\\right)^3}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   q^{2} = \\frac{q^{29}}{q^{27}} = q^{29-27} = q^{2}\n   \\]\n   Thus, $q^{2}$ is a positive rational number.\n\n5. It follows that \\( q \\) itself must be a positive rational number, because the square root (in a ratio of integers) of a positive rational number is also a positive rational number.\n\n6. Assume $q = \\frac{m}{n}$, where $m$ and $n$ are coprime natural numbers.\n\n7. The 30th term of the progression is given by:\n   \\[\n   a_{30} = a_{1} q^{29} = a_{1} \\left( \\frac{m}{n} \\right)^{29} = \\frac{a_{1} m^{29}}{n^{29}}\n   \\]\n   Since $a_{30}$ is a natural number, it implies that $a_{1} m^{29} / n^{29}$ is a natural number. Thus, $a_{1}$ must be divisible by $n^{29}$.\n\n8. Similarly, the 20th term of the progression is:\n   \\[\n   a_{20} = a_{1} q^{19} = a_{1} \\left( \\frac{m}{n} \\right)^{19} = \\frac{a_{1} m^{19}}{n^{19}}\n   \\]\n   As $a_{20}$ must be a natural number, and $a_{1}$ is divisible by $n^{29}$, $\\frac{a_{1} m^{19}}{n^{19}}$ is also a natural number because $n^{19}$ is a divisor of $a_{1} (which includes $n^{29}$).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nTherefore, the 20th term of the given geometric progression must also be a natural number.\n\\[\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given that a positive integer \\( A \\) can be factorized as \\( A = 2^{\\alpha} \\times 3^{\\beta} \\times 5^{\\gamma} \\), where \\( \\alpha \\), \\( \\beta \\), and \\( \\gamma \\) are natural numbers. If half of \\( A \\) is a perfect square, one-third of \\( A \\) is a perfect cube, and one-fifth of \\