inant.\n   - Combine terms over a common denominator \\( x_1x_2x_3 \\):\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \\frac{(x_1 - x_2)(x_2 - x_3)(x_3 - x_1)}{x_1x_2x_3} \\right|\n     \\]\n\n4. **Signed Area Consideration**: The given formula omits the absolute value, implying it represents the **signed area** based on the orientation of the points. The magnitude matches the conventional area.\n\n**Final Answer**:\n\\[\n\\boxed{t = \\frac{\\left(x_{1} - x_{2}\\right)\\left(x_{2} - x_{3}\\right)\\left(x_{3} - x_{1}\\right)}{2 x_{1} x_{2} x_{3}}}\n\\]\n\nThis formula accounts for the signed area, and the absolute value yields the geometric area.", "ground_truth_solution": "To find the area \\( t \\) of the triangle formed by the points \\( P_1(x_1, y_1), P_2(x_2, y_2), \\) and \\( P_3(x_3, y_3) \\) on the curve \\( xy = 1 \\):\n\n1. **Determine the expression for the area of the triangle**:\n   The area \\( t \\) of a triangle given by vertices \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\) can be expressed using the determinant of a matrix:\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\\n   x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\\n   x_3 & y_3 & 1\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute the curve equation \\( xy = 1 \\)**:\n   Since the points lie on the curve \\( xy = 1 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   y_i = \\frac{1}{x_i} \\quad \\text{for} \\; i=1,2,3\n   \\]\n   Thus, the determinant becomes:\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   x_1 & \\frac{1}{x_1} & 1 \\\\\n   x_2 & \\frac{1}{x_2} & 1 \\\\\n   x_3 & \\frac{1}{x_3} & 1\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the determinant**:\n   Multiply the first column by \\( x_1, x_2, \\) and \\( x_3 \\) respectively:\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   x_1^2 & 1 & x_1 \\\\\n   x_2^2 & 1 & x_2 \\\\\n   x_3^2 & 1 & x_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   \\]\n   Factor out \\( x_1 x_2 x_3 \\) from the denominator:\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{1}{2 x_1 x_2 x_3} \\left| \n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   x_1^2 & 1 & x_1 \\\\\n   x_2^2 & 1 & x_2 \\\\\n   x_3^2 & 1 & x_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   \\]\n\n4. **Rearrange the columns for simplicity**:\n   Rearrange the columns so that it resembles a better-known determinant form:\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{1}{2 x_1 x_2 x_3} \\left| \n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   1 & x_1 & x_1^2 \\\\\n   1 & x_2 & x_2^2 \\\\\n   1 & x_3 & x_3^2\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify using properties of determinants**:\n   Subtract the third row from the first and second rows respectively:\n   \\[\n   \\left|\n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   0 & x_1 - x_3 & x_1^2 - x_3^2 \\\\\n   0 & x_2 - x_3 & x_2^2 - x_3^2 \\\\\n   1 & x_3 & x_3^2\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   = \\left|\n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   0 & x_1 - x_3 & (x_1 - x_3)(x_1+x_3) \\\\\n   0 & x_2 - x_3 & (x_2 - x_3)(x_2+x_3) \\\\\n   1 & x_3 & x_3^2\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = (x_1 - x_3)(x_2 - x_3) \\left| \n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   0 & 1 & x_1 + x_3 \\\\\n   0 & 1 & x_2 + x_3 \\\\\n   1 & x_3 & x_3^2\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   \\]\n   Expand via cofactors along the first row:\n   \\[\n   \\left|\n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   1 & x_3 \\\\\n   1 & x_2 + x_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   = x_2 + x_3 - x_3 = x_2\n   \\]\n\n6. **Complete the calculations**:\n   \\[\n   \\left| \n   \\begin{array}{ccc}\n   0 & 0 & x_1 - x_3 \\\\\n   0 & 1 & x_2 + x_3 \\\\\n   1 & x_3 & x_3^2\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right|\n   = (x_{1}-x_{3})(x_{2}-x_{3})(x_{3}-x_{1})\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area \\( t \\) becomes:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{(x_{1}-x_{2})(x_{2}-x_{3})(x_{3}-x_{1})}{2 x_{1} x_{2} x_{3}}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{(x_{1}-x_{2})(x_{2}-x_{3})(x_{3}-x_{1})}{2 x_{1} x_{2} x_{3}}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $A B C$ be a triangle with transversal lines emanating from vertices $B