er (pair \\((i, j)\\)) belongs to exactly 2 commissions: \\( K_i \\) and \\( K_j \\).\n\n   - No two commissions share more than one member. This is clear since a pair (member) intersects exactly two commissions.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nGiven these constructions and verifications, the largest possible value of \\( m \\) that satisfies all given conditions is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2n-1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nGiven five points inside an equilateral triangle of area 1, show that one can find three equilateral triangles with total area at most 0.64 and sides parallel to the original triangle, so that each of the five points is inside one or more of the new triangles.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem here: given five points inside an equilateral triangle of area 1, I need to show that there exist three smaller equilateral triangles with total area at most 0.64, each with sides parallel to the original triangle, such that each of the five points is inside at least one of these new triangles. Hmm, interesting. Let me try to break this down.\n\nFirst, the original triangle is equilateral with area 1. The three smaller triangles we need to find must also be equilateral, with sides parallel to the original. That probably means they are scaled-down versions, positioned somewhere inside the original. Each of the five points must be covered by at least one of these three triangles. The total area of these three triangles should not exceed 0.64. \n\nSo, the challenge is to place three triangles in such a way that they cover all five points, and their combined area is as small as possible, specifically \u22640.64. Since the original triangle has area 1, 0.64 is a significant portion, but maybe there's a clever way to partition or arrange the three triangles to minimize the area while covering all points.\n\nLet me recall some related concepts. This seems similar to covering points wit