0b2 - (-c\u00b2)\n\n=> 2ga + 2gc = -a\u00b2 + c\u00b2\n\nFactor: 2g(a + c) = (c\u00b2 - a\u00b2) = (c - a)(c + a)\n\nAssuming a + c \u2260 0, which it isn't since a and c are positive.\n\nThus, 2g = (c - a)(c + a)/(a + c) = c - a\n\nTherefore, g = (c - a)/2\n\nSimilarly, from equations (2) and (4):\n\nFrom (2): 2fb + h = -b\u00b2\n\nFrom (4): -2fd + h = -d\u00b2\n\nSubtract (4) from (2):\n\n2fb + h - (-2fd + h) = -b\u00b2 - (-d\u00b2)\n\n=> 2fb + 2fd = -b\u00b2 + d\u00b2\n\nFactor: 2f(b + d) = d\u00b2 - b\u00b2 = (d - b)(d + b)\n\nAssuming b + d \u2260 0,\n\nThus, 2f = (d - b)(d + b)/(d + b) = d - b\n\nTherefore, f = (d - b)/2\n\nNow, substitute g and f into equation (1):\n\nFrom (1): 2ga + h = -a\u00b2\n\nWe have g = (c - a)/2, so:\n\n2*((c - a)/2)*a + h = -a\u00b2\n\n=> (c - a)a + h = -a\u00b2\n\n=> h = -a\u00b2 - (c - a)a = -a\u00b2 - ca + a\u00b2 = -ca\n\nSimilarly, from equation (2): 2fb + h = -b\u00b2\n\nf = (d - b)/2, so:\n\n2*((d - b)/2)*b + h = -b\u00b2\n\n=> (d - b)b + h = -b\u00b2\n\n=> h = -b\u00b2 - (d - b)b = -b\u00b2 - db + b\u00b2 = -db\n\nTherefore, we have h = -ca = -db => ca = db\n\nSo, in order for the four points to lie on a circle, the product of the lengths of the segments of one diagonal (ca) must equal the product of the lengths of the segments of the other diagonal (db). So EA * EC = EB * ED, which is a known property of cyclic quadrilaterals. So that checks out.\n\nTherefore, in this coordinate system, the circle has equation x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + 2gx + 2fy + h = 0, where g = (c - a)/2, f = (d - b)/2, h = -ca = -db.\n\nBut maybe this is getting too complicated. Let me see if there's another approach. The problem gives AB = 7, CE = 3, angle ADB = \u03b1. Wait, CE is the length from C to E, which in the coordinate system above is the distance from C(-c,0) to E(0,0), so CE = c = 3. Therefore, c = 3.\n\nGiven that, from the earlier relation, ca = db, and c = 3, so 3a = db.\n\nAlso, AB = 7. AB is the distance from A(a,0) to B(0,b), which is sqrt(