=10:10 + sqrt(10/3)+0.5\u224810 +1.826 +0.5\u224812.326, floor 12.\n\nn=11:11 + sqrt(11/3)+0.5\u224811 +1.915 +0.5\u224813.415, floor 13.\n\nn=12:12 + sqrt(4) +0.5=12 +2 +0.5=14.5, floor 14. f(12)=14.\n\nn=13:13 + sqrt(13/3)+0.5\u224813 +2.081 +0.5\u224815.581, floor 15.\n\nn=14:14 + sqrt(14/3)+0.5\u224814 +2.160 +0.5\u224816.660, floor 16.\n\nn=15:15 + sqrt(5) +0.5\u224815 +2.236 +0.5\u224817.736, floor 17.\n\nn=16:16 + sqrt(16/3)+0.5\u224816 +2.309 +0.5\u224818.809, floor 18.\n\nn=17:17 + sqrt(17/3)+0.5\u224817 +2.380 +0.5\u224819.880, floor 19.\n\nn=18:18 + sqrt(6) +0.5\u224818 +2.449 +0.5\u224820.949, floor 20. So f(18)=20.\n\nn=19:19 + sqrt(19/3)+0.5\u224819 +2.516 +0.5\u224822.016, floor 22. So here, between n=18 and n=19, we jump from 20 to 22, so 21 is missing, which is a_3=21. So that seems consistent. So the function skips 1,8,21,... as claimed. So this gives some numerical evidence that the claim is true. Now, how to prove it?\n\nI need to show two things:\n\n1. For every positive integer m not in the sequence a_n=3n\u00b2-2n, there exists some integer k such that f(k)=m.\n\n2. For every m in the sequence a_n, there is no integer k such that f(k)=m.\n\nAlternatively, maybe show that when m is of the form 3n\u00b2 -2n, then there is no k with f(k)=m, and otherwise, there is such a k.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps analyze the equation [k + sqrt(k/3) + 1/2] = m and find for which m there is a solution k, and show that the m without solutions are exactly those of the form 3n\u00b2 -2n.\n\nSo, let's consider the equation [k + sqrt(k/3) + 1/2] = m. This is equivalent to m \u2264 k + sqrt(k/3) + 1/2 < m + 1.\n\nWhich can be rewritten as m - 1/2 - sqrt(k/3) \u2264 k < m + 1/2 - sqrt(k/3).\n\nBut this seems a bit circular because k is on both sides. Maybe we can rearrange the inequality.\n\nLet me denote x = sqrt(k/3). Then x\u00b2 = k/3, so k=3x\u00b2.\n\nSubstituting back, the equation becomes:\n\nm \u2264 3x\u00b2 + x + 1/2 < m + 1.\n\nSo we have 3x\u00b2 + x + 1/2