icts sqrt(x +23)=m\u221a2 being positive. Therefore, y=-1 is invalid. So only y=1 is valid.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is y=1, x=9.\n\nWait, but let's verify with y=-1.\n\nIf y=-1, then according to the equation:\n\nsqrt(x - sqrt(x +23)) =2\u221a2 - (-1)=2\u221a2 +1\n\nBut the left side must be non-negative. The right side is 2\u221a2 +1\u22483.828, which is positive. Then, squaring both sides:\n\nx - sqrt(x +23) = (2\u221a2 +1)^2=8 +4\u221a2 +1=9 +4\u221a2\n\nTherefore, x - sqrt(x +23)=9 +4\u221a2\n\nBut from m=4y=-4, x=2*(-4)^2 -23=32 -23=9. So x=9.\n\nSo substituting x=9:\n\nLeft side: 9 - sqrt(9 +23)=9 - sqrt(32)=9 -4\u221a2\n\nBut the equation would require 9 -4\u221a2=9 +4\u221a2, which implies -4\u221a2=4\u221a2 \u21d2 -8\u221a2=0, which is impossible. Therefore, y=-1 is not a valid solution, even though algebraically we arrived at y=\u00b11. The reason is that m must be non-negative, so y must be positive. Therefore, only y=1 is valid.\n\nTherefore, the only integer solution is (x, y)=(9,1).\n\nWait, but earlier when we tried y=0, we got x non-integer. And here with y=1, we get a valid solution. Let me check if there are other possible values.\n\nWait, earlier, when we set m=4y, and found y=\u00b11, but since m must be non-negative, y must be positive. So y=1 is the only possible solution. Hence, only one pair (9,1) satisfies the equation.\n\nBut let me check if there's another way. Maybe by not substituting a variable and going back to the original equation.\n\nOriginal equation: sqrt(x - sqrt(x +23))=2\u221a2 - y.\n\nSuppose we set z = sqrt(x +23). Then z must be a non-negative real number, and x = z\u00b2 -23. Then, substituting into the equation:\n\nsqrt(z\u00b2 -23 - z) =2\u221a2 - y\n\nSquare both sides:\n\nz\u00b2 - z -23 = (2\u221a2 - y)^2=8 -4\u221a2 y +y\u00b2\n\nThus,\n\nz\u00b2 - z -23 = y\u00b2 -4\u221a2 y +8\n\nRearranged:\n\nz\u00b2 - z -23 - y\u00b2 +4\u221a2 y -8=0 \u21d2z\u00b2 - z - y\u00b2 -31 +4\u221a2 y=0\n\nBut z is sqrt(x +23), w